nce of a Roman dominating function, where the roman weight equals the Roman domination number will be investigated.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing Roman Domination Number Constraints**\\n\\nExamining the impossibility of `romanWeight f ≤ radius G`. Initially considering cases for the set of vertices labeled 2, denoted S. If S is empty, deduce a contradiction via `card_le_romanWeight_of_no_two`.\\n\\nNext, the case where S contains a single vertex is evaluated, leading to another contradiction using `radius_add_one_le_romanWeight_of_unique_two`. Then, explored the case where the cardinality of S is greater or equal to 2, leading to $\\\\text{radius } G \\\\ge 4$ and $\\\\text{romanWeight } f \\\\ge 4$.\\n\\nConsidering the case of |S| = 2. Recognizing every vertex labeled 0 must be adjacent to an element in S. Every vertex outside of the function's labeled vertices (V1) is within distance 1 of S.\\n\\n\\n**Constructing Proof by Contradiction**\\n\\nDetermining the length of path $P$ between $u_1$ and $u_2$, the intermediate vertices and their relationship to $V_1$ is key. This path length is at most $3 + |V_1|$. Calculating the distances between a midpoint $v^*$ on path $P$ and any vertex x in V gives a bound on eccentricity.\\n\\nThe eccentricity of $v^*$ is derived, bounding $\\\\text{radius}(G)$ by  $\\\\text{romanWeight}(f) - 1$.  Contradiction arises from $\\\\text{romanWeight}(f) \\\\le \\\\text{radius}(G)$, so the contradiction implies the initial assumption must be false.\\n\\nGeneralizing with |S| = m, the bound $2m + |V_1| - 1$ leads to radius(G) $\\\\le$ romanWeight(f) - 1, thereby deriving a contradiction and completing the proof strategy.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"run_command\",\"args\":{\"CommandLine\":\"\\\"cat << 'EOF' > /mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/test_full.lean\\\\nimport Mathlib\\\\nimport LeanProject.GraphInvariantsComputable\\\\n\\\\nopen SimpleGraph\\\\nopen GraphCalc\\\\n\\\\n-- Copy of Chal